18. Attack breakers

Because we do not know initial access, divide these into general blast-radius controls.

Breaker 1 — Tenant segmentation

Compromise of one service shouldn't expose every customer's records.

Breaker 2 — JIT privileged access

No permanent broad customer-data access.

Breaker 3 — Multi-customer anomaly detection

Alert when one identity suddenly crosses many customer boundaries.

Breaker 4 — Bulk enumeration detection

File metadata and directory enumeration deserve monitoring.

Breaker 5 — Egress monitoring

Detect unusual outbound data volumes and destinations.

Breaker 6 — DLP

Sensitive customer/employee content leaving approved pathways should generate alerts.

Breaker 7 — Immutable audit trail

Attackers shouldn't be able to erase evidence of which customer datasets were accessed.

Breaker 8 — Retention reduction

Remove historical files that no longer need to exist.

Breaker 9 — Customer-scoped encryption

Where practical, customer-specific keys can reduce cross-tenant blast radius.

Breaker 10 — Recovery-independent security administration

Separate production access from security logging and forensic evidence stores.

